## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to blend BFS intuition with shortest-path techniques under a hop constraint and to reconstruct a path from augmented state.

## Key Concepts
- Augmented state graph: (node, hops)
- Dijkstra with 2D dist and predecessor tracking
- Path reconstruction from (node, hops) chain
- Time/space: O((N*H + M*H) log(N*H))

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathWithHops(graph, S, T, H) {
  // graph: {u: [{to,w}, ...], ...}
  const dist = new Map();
  const prev = new Map();
  // min-heap assumed available: push({time, node, hops})
  const heap = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.time-b.time);
  dist.set(`${S}|0`, 0);
  heap.push({time:0, node:S, hops:0});
  while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
    const {time,u,k} = heap.pop();
    if (time !== dist.get(`${u}|${k}`)) continue;
    for (const {to:v, w} of (graph[u] || [])) {
      const nk = k + 1;
      if (nk > H) continue;
      const nd = time + w;
      const key = `${v}|${nk}`;
      if (nd < (dist.get(key) ?? Infinity)) {
        dist.set(key, nd);
        prev.set(key, `${u}|${k}`);
        heap.push({time: nd, node: v, hops: nk});
      }
    }
  }
  let bestTime = Infinity; let bestKey = null;
  for (let k = 0; k <= H; k++) {
    const key = `${T}|${k}`;
    const d = dist.get(key);
    if (d != null && d < bestTime) { bestTime = d; bestKey = key; }
  }
  const path = [];
  if (bestKey) {
    let cur = bestKey;
    while (cur) {
      const [node] = cur.split('|');
      path.push(node);
      cur = prev.get(cur);
    }
    path.reverse();
  }
  return { time: bestTime, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple equal shortest paths?
- How would you optimize memory for very large graphs?
- How would you adapt if hops constraint is on edges vs. nodes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of robust sorting strategies used in production libraries. Combines quicksort behavior, 3-way partitioning for duplicates, and a guaranteed worst-case fallback with heapsort. Assesses pivot choice, in-place constraints, and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort with depth-based fallback to heapsort
- 3-way partitioning: <, =, > pivot to handle duplicates efficiently
- Depth limit: d = floor(2*log2(n)) prevents O(n^2) degeneration
- In-place implementation with O(log n) recursion overhead

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const depthLimit = n > 1 ? Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n)) : 0;

  function swap(i, j) {
    const t = arr[i]; arr[i] = arr[j]; arr[j] = t;
  }

  function heapSortSub(start, end) {
    const len = end - start;
    function heapify(i, heapSize) {
      let largest = i;
      while (true) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1;
        const r = l + 1;
        let idx = i;
        if (l < heapSize && arr[start + l] > arr[start + idx]) idx = l;
        if (r < heapSize && arr[start + r] > arr[start + idx]) idx = r;
        if (idx !== i) { [arr[start + i], arr[start + idx]] = [arr[start + idx], arr[start + i]]; i = idx; } else break;
      }
    }
    for (let i = Math.floor(len / 2) - 1; i >= 0; i--) heapify(i, len);
    for (let endIdx = len - 1; endIdx > 0; endIdx--) {
      swap(start, start + endIdx);
      heapify(0, endIdx);
    }
  }

  function quicksort3(start, end, depth) {
    if (end - start <= 1) return;
    if (depth <= 0) {
      heapSortSub(start, end);
      return;
    }
    const pivot = arr[start + Math.floor((start + end - 1) / 2)];
    let lt = start, i = start, gt = end - 1;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { swap(lt, i); lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { swap(i, gt); gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    quicksort3(start, lt, depth - 1);
    quicksort3(gt + 1, end, depth - 1);
  }

  quicksort3(0, n, depthLimit);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for parallel sorting on large arrays?
- What changes if stability is required while keeping O(n log n) average time?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to design a compact prefix-search data structure and implement tie-breaking logic using a Trie.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequencies stored at each node
- Prefix search via traversal to the prefix end node
- DFS collection of all words in the subtree
- Sorting by frequency (descending), then lexicographic order for ties

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.freq = 0;
  }
}

class Autocomplete {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  addWord(word, freq) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.freq = freq;
  }

  getSuggestions(prefix, limit) {
    if (limit <= 0) return [];

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    const results = [];
    const dfs = (currentNode, path) => {
      if (currentNode.freq > 0) {
        results.push({ word: path, freq: currentNode.freq });
      }
      for (const [char, childNode] of currentNode.children) {
        dfs(childNode, path + char);
      }
    };

    dfs(node, prefix);

    results.sort((a, b) => {
      if (b.freq !== a.freq) return b.freq - a.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });

    return results.slice(0, limit).map(r => r.word);
  }
}
```

Why This Is Asked\n\nThis question probes understanding of practical sorting trade-offs beyond textbook asymptotics, including cache locality, in-place constraints, and robust pivot strategies. It invites discussion of 3-way partitioning to handle duplicates, median-of-three to curb worst-case behavior, and a small insertion sort for tiny partitions.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- 3-way partitioning in quicksort\n- median-of-three pivot selection\n- insertion sort threshold tuning\n- in-place algorithms and cache efficiency\n- iterative vs recursive implementations\n\n## Code Example\n```javascript
function sortInPlace(arr){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(a,i,j)=>{const t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  const medianOfThree=(a,i,j,k)=>{let x=[a[i],i],[a[j],j],[a[k],k].sort((p,q)=>p[0]-q[0]); return a[x[1]];};
  // iterative 3-way quicksort skeleton
  const stack=[[0,arr.length-1]];
  while(stack.length){
    const [lo,hi]=stack.pop();
    if(hi-lo+1<=TH){ // insertion sort for small partitions
      for(let i=lo+1;i<=hi;i++){
        let t=arr[i], j=i-1;
        while(j>=lo && arr[j]>t){ arr[j+1]=arr[j]; j-- }
        arr[j+1]=t;
      }
      continue;
    }
    // median-of-three pivot
    const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
    const pivot= medianOfThree(arr, lo, mid, hi);
    // 3-way partition
    let lt=lo, i=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(arr[i]<pivot) swap(arr, lt++, i++);
      else if(arr[i]>pivot) swap(arr, i, gt--);
      else i++;
    }
    stack.push([lo, lt-1]);
    stack.push([gt+1, hi]);
  }
}
```

